October 4th, 1774

Today Bart and I were able to play some chess in the station while Mongo was held captive thanks to
Bart's Candygram. Luckily, my skills are still sharp from when I was known more often as the Waco Kid.

The sun burned hot over Rock Ridge, and yet, the townspeople carried on — rebuilding, repainting, repairing.
I sat by the window for a moment and thought about all that had come before. It’s funny how chaos gives birth 
to peace, even if only for a spell.

At one point, Bart nudged me mid-game and said: “Sometimes, the answers are in plain sight, Waco.”
He was right. That chessboard had something funny about it. Turns out, every piece we moved spelled something out.

I replayed the moves in my mind: e2 to e4, d7 to d5, Nf3, Nc6, Bb5...

Strange sequence for casual play. Almost like Bart wanted me to see it. And I did.

The moves — in order — matched a pattern we used back in Cheyenne. A code. Simple binary.
I translated it later that night, just before lights out. The final sequence, once decoded, read:

**clayFlag{my_name_is_jim_but_most_people_call_me_jim}**

If someone finds this, keep riding. Lamarr ain’t done scheming yet, and there are whispers he’s taken 
to the digital trails now.

- Jim “The Waco Kid”
